On Mon, 21 Jun 2004 12:15:38 -0700, metalive wrote:

> I'm afraid I screwed up the last (4th) level of Sokoban. I'm missing only
> one more boulder to fill the last remaining hole... but it's in a corner
> :-(

 Zap it with a wand of teleport, or read one of the scrolls of earth you
find on the first level of sokoban to make nine more boulders. The latter
will incur a small luck penalty, and some hitpoint loss from the middle
boulder when it conks you on the head. :)


> 1. If I risk reading a scroll or quaffing a potion I usually instantly
> notice some effect (allthough often I don't know what the vague
> description message of what just happened actually triggered). But let's
> say a certain scroll makes my hands glow red. Then I would want to name it
> for example "scroll of red glowing hands", in order that my custom name
> replaces the cryptic YUM YUM DWARA DEIDA BLA BLA crap. But how can I name
> a scroll or potion, that has just dissappered after use?

 If you haven't named it, most will ask for a name or id themselves. You
might name it something specific before reading it, but generally I'd try
to avoid reading scrolls if you've no clue what they are. A scroll of fire
or amnesia can be nasty.

> 2. Does worn armor "weigh" something?

 Yes.

> 3. Does gold "weigh" something?

 Very little.

> 4. How can one repair corroded items?

 A potion of oil will remove rust. Being confused while enchanting it is
better.

> 5. How does one remove cursed armor or rings, etc.?

 Usually a scroll of remove curse, or enchant armor or weapon. A
potion of holy water will remove the curse, just make sure you keep
one potion. You can also pray to your deity if you haven't bothered him in
a while. Monkeys or nymphs might be induced to steal them off you if you
drop the other stuff they might grab instead. Extreme measures might be to
polymorph yourself into something with no hands.


> 6. Have those occaisional "dark" rooms any special meaning?

 Yes. You can't see in them. ^_^

> 7. How does one dig whole into the ground with pick-axes?

 Apply your pickaxe, when asked for the direction, say ">". That indicates
downwards, "." indicates at yourself, "<" indicates up.


> 8. I think there is a strong lack of scrolls of identify in the game. Only
> in a few games I was lucky enough, that the first few shops were of the
> "bookstore"-type and that I could buy identify scrolls. But most of the
> time I find myself accumulating tons and tons of rings amulets and gems,
> hoping that at one point I will be able to find out what they are and
> actually benefit from them. I think not being able to use magic items
> misses the whole point of RPGs. How do you guys tackle this?

 That's one of the primary obstacles in nethack. You can group things by
price for starters. (identify is the cheapest scroll, light is next,
enchant weapon costs the same as blank paper, remove curse and enchant
armor are next, etc)
 Drop stuff on an altar to see if they're blessed or cursed, and then you
can safely try on most rings for a turn or two to see if they do
anything obvious. (leaving the very expensive polymorph ring on too long
may be bad for you!)
 Engrave something in the floor with your fingers (use "-" for what to
write with) and then try engraving with your wands to see what they are -
almost all the important ones can be worked out from the hints this gives,
although it uses a charge. E.g., if the writing vanishes, your wand's
either teleport or cancellation, and you should keep it away from your bag
of holding until you know which is which. :)
 Try (a)pplying potions to find the potion of oil - it can be lit and
thrown. Apply it again to snuff it out. Try dipping arrows or darts into
them to find sickness (poisons it) and polymorph. Name them before hand,
however, cause they'll be used up.
 There are lots of little tricks you can do to figure out what things are,
or at least might be, in nethack.
 
